    </owl:Restriction>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000049"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:nodeID="genid769"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>type 2 diabetes</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3176767/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Participants in the investigation (Table 1) were from two studies of South Asian individuals; the United Kingdom Asian Diabetes Study (UKADS; 857 participants with type 2 diabetes, 417 without) [2], [3] and the Diabetes Genetics in Pakistan study (DGP; 821 participants with type 2 diabetes, 1167 without) [3].</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000049"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Pakistan"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3317653/</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Pakistan-based subjects (840 with type 2 diabetes, 1309 normoglycaemic individuals) were recruited from the Mirpur region of Azad Kashmir</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000049"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST001213</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Genome-wide association study in individuals of South Asian ancestry identifies six new type 2 diabetes susceptibility loci.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:21874001</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3773920/</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Replication testing amongst South Asians was carried out amongst 13,170 T2D cases and 25,398 controls participants from the following studies: the Chennai Urban Rural Epidemiology Study (CURES)36 the COBRA study37 the Diabetes Genetics in Pakistan (DGP) and UK Asian Diabetes Study (UKADS),38, 39</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000049"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:21949744</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Effects of 16 genetic variants on fasting glucose and type 2 diabetes in South Asians: ADCY5 and GLIS3 variants may predispose to type 2 diabetes.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:21949744</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/research/bionlp/RESTful/pmcoa.cgi/BioC_xml/PMC3176767/unicode</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Diabetes Genetics in Pakistan study (DGP; 821 participants with type 2 diabetes, 1167 without)</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000049"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A case-control collection of people of Punjabi ancestry, predominantly originating from Mirpur, Pakistan, with type 2 diabetes or normal glucose regulation, assembled to investigate genetic susceptibility to diabetes and related glycaemic traits.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:21949744</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Fable 5.1 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
        <dcterms:contributor>GPT-6 (OpenAI Codex), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
