        <obo:IAO_0000115>A population-based biobank in the most northern part of Germany, run from Kiel University, that recruits patients through complete ascertainment in a geographically confined area together with population controls, covering cardiovascular, neuropsychiatric and environmental diseases, to estimate population-specific genotypic risks for complex diseases.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Biobank PopGen</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Biobank popgen</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>KIEL</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>POPGEN</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PopGen</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PopGen cohort</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>The Population Genetic Cohort</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">PopGen Biobank</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000406"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Germany"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A16490960+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The PopGen project uses the most northern part of Germany as a target region for such a pursuit.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000406"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90257012</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>A genome-wide association study in autoimmune neurological syndromes with anti-GAD65 autoantibodies.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:35348614</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC9976967/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The control population used in this study consisted of a cohort of 1081 individuals, part of the PopGen cohort, a population-based biobank in Germany.8⁠ This subset of individuals corresponded to healthy subjects without neurological or endocrine disease, age-matched to our anti-GAD65 AINS patients, with available genotype data generated on the Infinium Global Screening Array (Illumina).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
