        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Howard University Family Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000476"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC8499470/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The HUFS (Principal Investigator: CNR) was a study of AA from the Washington DC metropolitan area (USA) and was designed to investigate the genetic and environmental basis of common complex traits such as hypertension, obesity, and T2D [32].</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000476"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90091134</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Genome-wide analyses of multiple obesity-related cytokines and hormones informs biology of cardiometabolic traits.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:34620218</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC8499470/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Replication analyses were performed using data on 7990 AA from five different cohorts: the Howard University Family Study (HUFS) [32]</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000476"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A population-based family study of African Americans from the Washington, DC metropolitan area, enrolling randomly ascertained families and unrelated individuals through Howard University without selection on any phenotype, to study the genetic and environmental basis of common complex diseases including hypertension, obesity and type 2 diabetes.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC2702100/fullTextXML</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC8499470/fullTextXML</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000476"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/numberOfParticipants"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1976</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>analysed subset: genotyped participants (pedigree members and unrelated)</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC4684912/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Methods We analyzed 1,976 African Americans from Washington, D.C, including 1,322 individuals from 328 pedigrees and 654 unrelated individuals, enrolled in the Howard University Family Study.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000476"/>
