    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000578"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/China"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://static-content.springer.com/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41398-025-03418-z/MediaObjects/41398_2025_3418_MOESM1_ESM.pdf</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Shanghai Men’s Health Study (SMHS) is an ongoing population-based cohort study of 61,480 Chinese men who were aged between 40 and 74 years, were free of cancer at enrollment, and lived in urban Shanghai, China</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000578"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90570660</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Multi-ancestry genome-wide association analyses incorporating SNP-by-psychosocial interactions identify novel loci for serum lipids.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:40537477</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://static-content.springer.com/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41398-025-03418-z/MediaObjects/41398_2025_3418_MOESM1_ESM.pdf</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Shanghai Men’s Health Study (SMHS) is an ongoing population-based cohort study of 61,480 Chinese men who were aged between 40 and 74 years, were free of cancer at enrollment, and lived in urban Shanghai, China; 45,766 (74.4%) provided a blood samples.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000578"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A population-based prospective cohort of 61,480 men aged 40–74 living in urban Shanghai, recruited from 2002 with detailed lifestyle, dietary and medical data and blood, urine and DNA samples, and followed by in-person surveys every 3–4 years and linkage with cancer and vital statistics registries, to study lifestyle, environmental and genetic contributions to cancer and other non-communicable diseases.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:25733578</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000578"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/numberOfParticipants"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">61480</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>men aged 40-74 enrolled 2002-2006</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://europepmc.org/api/getPdf?pmcid=PMC4521127</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>By June 2006, 83033 potential eligible men had been approached for the study; 61480 were enrolled, an overall response rate of 74.0%.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000578"/>
