        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90833020</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Multi ancestry genome wide association meta analysis of urinary aMT6s levels.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:42020658</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC13269534/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>To address these limitations, we aggregated data from the Taiwan Biobank (TWB), the Nurses’ Health Study I (NHS1), the Nurses’ Health Study II (NHS2), Osteoporotic Fractures in Men19,20 (MrOS), and the Multiethnic Cohort (MEC).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000619"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A prospective cohort of 116,429 female registered nurses aged 25–42 recruited in 1989 from 14 states of the United States, a younger population than the original Nurses&apos; Health Study, followed by biennial mailed questionnaires and biospecimen collection to study risk factors for major chronic diseases in women.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:34450027</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:38677085</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000619"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/numberOfParticipants"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">116429</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>female registered nurses aged 25-42 recruited in 1989</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC12225639/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Started in 1989, NHSII recruited 116,429 female registered nurses ages 25–42 from 14 states with large numbers of registered nurses.</rdfs:comment>
