        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90841457</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A prospective prebirth cohort of about 1,000 pregnant women aged 18 or over recruited in the first trimester at the pregnancy blood-sampling clinic in Sherbrooke, Canada, between 2010 and 2013, and their children, followed to study the biological, environmental and genetic determinants of glucose regulation in pregnancy and its effects on fetal development and offspring health.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <coho:numberOfParticipants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1024</coho:numberOfParticipants>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Gen3G</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Genetics of Glycemic regulation in Gestation and Growth</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Genetics of Glucose regulation in Gestation and Growth</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Restriction rdf:nodeID="genid5913">
        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/is_population_study_of"/>
        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0005406"/>
    </owl:Restriction>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000655"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:nodeID="genid5913"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>gestational diabetes mellitus</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A40122564+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Initiated in 2010, the Genetics of Glucose regulation in Gestation and Growth (Gen3G) prospective cohort investigates the pathophysiology of impaired glycaemic regulation in pregnancy and evaluates its impact on both the mothers and her offspring health trajectory.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000655"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Canada"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A26842272+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Genetics of Glucose regulation in Gestation and Growth (Gen3G): a prospective prebirth cohort of mother-child pairs in Sherbrooke, Canada.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000655"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90841457</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Multi-ancestry, trans-generational GWAS meta-analysis of gestational diabetes and glycaemic traits during pregnancy reveals limited evidence of pregnancy-specific genetic effects.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:42225636</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC13226718/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>EFSOCH (EUR), Gen3G (EUR), FinnGeDi (EUR), ALSPAC (EUR), HAPO (EUR, AFR, EAS, SAS, HIS) and ELGH (SAS).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000655"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A prospective prebirth cohort of about 1,000 pregnant women aged 18 or over recruited in the first trimester at the pregnancy blood-sampling clinic in Sherbrooke, Canada, between 2010 and 2013, and their children, followed to study the biological, environmental and genetic determinants of glucose regulation in pregnancy and its effects on fetal development and offspring health.</owl:annotatedTarget>
