        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90276149</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A population-based cohort of about 1,000 adults from the mainland city of Split, the second-largest city in Croatia, recruited within the 10,001 Dalmatians biobank project as an urban counterpart to the isolated island cohorts of Vis and Korčula, for studies of the genetic, environmental and social determinants of health and disease.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <coho:numberOfParticipants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1012</coho:numberOfParticipants>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CROATIA-SPLIT</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CROATIA-Split</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>CROATIA_Split</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Split sub-cohort</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">CROATIA-Split Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000663"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Croatia"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC6016867/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>This study was performed on samples from three Croatian populations: from the Dalmatian islands of Korcula and Vis and the mainland city of Split, within the large-scale project of “10,001 Dalmatians” (Rudan et al., 2009).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000663"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90276149</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Genome-Wide Meta-Analysis Identifies Multiple Novel Rare Variants to Predict Common Human Infectious Diseases Risk.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:37108169</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC10138356/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Here, we used the data from three sub-cohorts for which we had bacterial and viral infections disease data—Korčula (N = 2833), Vis (N = 1039) and Split (N = 1012).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000663"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A population-based cohort of about 1,000 adults from the mainland city of Split, the second-largest city in Croatia, recruited within the 10,001 Dalmatians biobank project as an urban counterpart to the isolated island cohorts of Vis and Korčula, for studies of the genetic, environmental and social determinants of health and disease.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC10138356/fullTextXML</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
