        <obo:IAO_0000116>gestational diabetes mellitus</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11076516/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>To better understand the mechanisms and improve prediction of APOs in nulliparous individuals, the Nulliparous Pregnancy Outcomes Study: Monitoring Mothers-to-Be (nuMoM2b) consortium9,10 recruited and prospectively followed a large cohort of nulliparous people beginning in their first trimester of pregnancy.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000877"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:nodeID="genid7999"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>adverse pregnancy outcomes</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11076516/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>To better understand the mechanisms and improve prediction of APOs in nulliparous individuals, the Nulliparous Pregnancy Outcomes Study: Monitoring Mothers-to-Be (nuMoM2b) consortium9,10 recruited and prospectively followed a large cohort of nulliparous people beginning in their first trimester of pregnancy.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000877"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:nodeID="genid8001"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>pregnancy loss</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11076516/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>To better understand the mechanisms and improve prediction of APOs in nulliparous individuals, the Nulliparous Pregnancy Outcomes Study: Monitoring Mothers-to-Be (nuMoM2b) consortium9,10 recruited and prospectively followed a large cohort of nulliparous people beginning in their first trimester of pregnancy.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000877"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC13511387/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Briefly, the nuMoM2b study was a prospective multicenter cohort study of 10,038 pregnant individuals with singleton gestations recruited from eight clinical sites in the US.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000877"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90429686</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Genetic polymorphisms associated with adverse pregnancy outcomes in nulliparas.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:38714721</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11076516/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The objective of this study was to test for association between common variants across the maternal genome and four APOs in the nuMoM2b cohort: gestational length (as a proxy for preterm birth), preeclampsia, gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM), and pregnancy loss.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000877"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A prospective multicentre US cohort of 10,038 nulliparous women with singleton pregnancies recruited in the first trimester at eight clinical sites between 2010 and 2013 and followed through pregnancy, to study the factors that contribute to adverse pregnancy outcomes such as preterm birth, preeclampsia and gestational diabetes.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:38714721</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:42557812</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000877"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/numberOfParticipants"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">10038</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>nulliparous pregnant individuals enrolled in the first trimester (2010-2013)</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:38714721</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The study enrolled 10,038 nulliparous people from the first trimester of their pregnancy to participate in three study visits during pregnancy.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000877"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>nuMoM2b</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000878 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000878">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Northern_America"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <coho:isSubCohortOf rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000489"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90053831</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A population-based study of youths aged 8–21 living in the greater Philadelphia area, United States, recruited through the Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia Center for Applied Genomics from non-psychiatric paediatric clinics and assessed with clinical, neurocognitive and neuroimaging measures to describe how genetics affects brain development and cognitive functioning in adolescence.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <coho:numberOfParticipants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">9428</coho:numberOfParticipants>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>PNC</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Philadelphia Neurodevelopmental Cohort</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000878"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC12671915/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Philadelphia Neurodevelopmental Cohort (PNC) is a population-based study of youth living in the greater Philadelphia (United States) area (Calkins et al., 2015) that aims to describe how genetics impacts brain development and cognitive functioning in adolescents (Satterthwaite et al., 2014).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000878"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/isSubCohortOf"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000489"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC8713563/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>All participants were recruited through the Center for Applied Genomics at The Children’s Hospital in Philadelphia.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000878"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90053831</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Genetic and demographic predisposing factors associated with pediatric sleepwalking in the Philadelphia Neurodevelopmental Cohort.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:34563919</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A34563919+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Data from the Philadelphia Neurodevelopmental Cohort (PNC) of 7515 youths aged between 8 and 22 years were used in analyses.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000878"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A population-based study of youths aged 8–21 living in the greater Philadelphia area, United States, recruited through the Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia Center for Applied Genomics from non-psychiatric paediatric clinics and assessed with clinical, neurocognitive and neuroimaging measures to describe how genetics affects brain development and cognitive functioning in adolescence.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:41287920</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000878"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/numberOfParticipants"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">9428</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>youths aged 8-21 who completed the clinical and neurocognitive assessments (2006-2012)</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:41287920</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Overall, 19,161 individuals met the inclusion criteria; 9,428 (49.2%) of these completed clinical and neurocognitive assessments.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000878"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PNC</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


