        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90502757</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Trans-ancestry genome-wide association study of childhood body mass index identifies novel loci and age-specific effects.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:39885687</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11875162/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Early Growth Genetics (EGG) Consortium is a collaboration of multiple GWAS efforts to identify genetic loci that impact multiple traits related to early growth.16 We obtained summary statistics from the most recently published childhood standardized BMIz GWAS16 (excluding participants from the Norwegian Mother and Child Cohort [MoBa]) for inclusion in our meta-analysis.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000954"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A consortium that facilitates joint analysis of genetic data from multiple studies to identify human genomic loci influencing traits related to early growth, from the fetus to young adulthood, such as birth weight and length, gestational duration, head circumference, childhood body mass index and obesity, and pubertal growth.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://egg-consortium.org/</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000954"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/numberOfParticipants"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">321223</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>individuals with own birth weight in the 2019 EGG fetal birth weight GWAS meta-analysis</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:31043758</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>In expanded genome-wide association analyses of own birth weight (n = 321,223) and offspring birth weight (n = 230,069 mothers), we identified 190 independent association signals (129 of which are novel).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
