        <coho:isCohortOf rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000105"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90451683</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A population-based cohort of adults descended from the inhabitants of the upper Val Borbera, a genetically isolated valley in the Apennine mountains of Piedmont, north-west Italy, recruited on the basis of their ancestry in the valley&apos;s villages and nearby areas, with clinical and anthropometric data collected by physicians of San Raffaele Hospital, Milan, to identify genes and variants underlying common disorders and quantitative traits; one of the isolated populations of the Italian Network of Genetic Isolates.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <coho:numberOfParticipants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1803</coho:numberOfParticipants>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>INGI-VB</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>INGI-Val_Borbera</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Italian Network on Genetic Isolates-Val Borbera</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>VB</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>VBI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Val Borbera</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Val Borbera cohort</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Val Borbera study</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#GWAS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">INGI-Val Borbera</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001091"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Italy"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC2761731/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The aim of the present study is to characterize the population isolate of the upper Val Borbera, a large valley localized within the Apennine mountains of Piedmont, in the Northwest of Italy, geographically secluded from the surrounding areas.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001091"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90451683</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Novel loci and biomedical consequences of iron homoeostasis variation.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:39643614</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11624196/supplementaryFiles</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The INGI‐Val Borbera (INGI-VB) study comprises 1,785 healthy participants enrolled in the Val Borbera Valley, a geographically isolated region located within the Apennine Mountains in Northwest Italy.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001091"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A population-based cohort of adults descended from the inhabitants of the upper Val Borbera, a genetically isolated valley in the Apennine mountains of Piedmont, north-west Italy, recruited on the basis of their ancestry in the valley&apos;s villages and nearby areas, with clinical and anthropometric data collected by physicians of San Raffaele Hospital, Milan, to identify genes and variants underlying common disorders and quantitative traits; one of the isolated populations of the Italian Network of Genetic Isolates.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:19847309</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:37174278</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001091"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/numberOfParticipants"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1803</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>adults recruited in Val Borbera (2009)</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:19847309</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>As a part of a research program aimed at the identification of genes and variants associated to common disorders, we present here the analysis of a cohort of 1803 adult subjects from Val Borbera, selected based on their ancestry to represent the general population in the valley.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
