    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001246"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:nodeID="genid11293"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>military service during the Vietnam era (1965–1975)</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11257108/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Briefly, VETSA includes 1608 men who were recruited from the Vietnam Era Twin Registry (VETR), a nationally representative registry, which includes male twins, who were in the military at some time during the Vietnam era (1965–1975).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001246"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:nodeID="genid11295"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>mild cognitive impairment</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/twin-research-and-human-genetics/article/current-status-of-the-vietnam-era-twin-study-of-aging-vetsa/F0C33CE51AC366CD436926EA2CE42092</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>VETSA began as a longitudinal behavioral genetic study of cognitive and brain aging, but as the participants have aged, its primary focus has shifted to early identification of risk for mild cognitive impairment (MCI) and Alzheimer’s disease (AD).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001246"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:nodeID="genid11297"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>Alzheimer&apos;s disease</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/twin-research-and-human-genetics/article/current-status-of-the-vietnam-era-twin-study-of-aging-vetsa/F0C33CE51AC366CD436926EA2CE42092</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>VETSA began as a longitudinal behavioral genetic study of cognitive and brain aging, but as the participants have aged, its primary focus has shifted to early identification of risk for mild cognitive impairment (MCI) and Alzheimer’s disease (AD).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001246"/>
