        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fng.1019/MediaObjects/41588_2012_BFng1019_MOESM23_ESM.pdf</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>SJTU SDIID/SDS Chinese 3410 3412 6822 SDIID: case-control, SDS: community-based</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001275"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>GCST90293085</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>A cross-ancestry genome-wide meta-analysis, fine-mapping, and gene prioritization approach to characterize the genetic architecture of adiponectin.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:37859345</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC10652123/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Leveraging summary statistics from diverse genetic studies, we conducted a cross-ancestry, genome-wide meta-analysis for adiponectin in up to 46,434 individuals from the Metabolic Syndrome in Men (METSIM) cohort and the Adiponectin Genetics (ADIPOGen) and Asian Genetic Epidemiology Network (AGEN) consortiums.4,5,15 Our primary objectives were to (1) discover previously unreported loci associated with plasma adiponectin levels, (2) narrow putative causal variants underlying the association signals, (3) prioritize target genes systematically by using our proposed Gene Priority Score (GPScore) approach based on evidence derived from 11 gene prioritization strategies and physical distance to</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001275"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A consortium of genetic epidemiology studies of type 2 diabetes and related traits in people of East Asian ancestry, mostly population-, hospital- or family-based cohorts in East and South-East Asia, which meta-analyses its member studies&apos; genome-wide association results in trait-specific groups (such as AGEN-T2D and AGEN-BP) for type 2 diabetes, glycaemic traits, adiposity, adiponectin, blood pressure and kidney function.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:22797727</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:23105927</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:24647736</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:32499647</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:37859345</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001275"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/numberOfCases"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">77418</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>cases and controls in the 2020 AGEN type 2 diabetes meta-analysis (23 GWAS)</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:32499647</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Identification of type 2 diabetes loci in 433,540 East Asian individuals.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
