        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3590244/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Individuals with a diagnosis of bipolar I disorder were identified by psychiatrists across Scotland.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001528"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:25229638</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Dysfunction of emotional brain systems in individuals at high risk of mood disorder with depression and predictive features prior to illness.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:25229638</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A25229638+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Bipolar Family Study (BFS) is a prospective longitudinal study that has examined individuals at familial risk of mood disorder and healthy controls on three occasions, 2 years apart.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001528"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A prospective longitudinal study based at the University of Edinburgh of young people aged 16–25 at entry who were well but at high familial risk of mood disorder, having at least one first-degree or two second-degree relatives with bipolar I disorder (identified through patients with bipolar I disorder referred by psychiatrists across Scotland), and of unrelated controls with no personal or family history of bipolar disorder drawn from their social networks, assessed clinically and with brain MRI on three occasions about two years apart to study changes that precede the onset of mood disorders.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:23483904</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:25229638</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:27418025</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:32954013</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001528"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/numberOfParticipants"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">283</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>adolescents and young adults (high-risk and controls) recruited to the SBFS</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:32954013</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Participants were adolescents and young adults (N = 283, age 15–30 years) recruited as part of the Scottish Bipolar Family Study (SBFS) (Chan et al., 2016; Ganzola et al., 2018; Sprooten et al., 2011; Whalley et al., 2015).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
