        <rdfs:comment>Control subjects were recruited through driver’s license records maintained by the Missouri Family Registry, housed at Washington University School of Medicine for research purposes.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001745"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PGS019953</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Alcohol is neither a risk factor nor a protective factor for sudden cardiac death and/or fatal ventricular arrhythmia: A population-based study with genetic traits and alcohol consumption in the UK Biobank.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:38697272</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001745"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:30482948</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Transancestral GWAS of alcohol dependence reveals common genetic underpinnings with psychiatric disorders.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:30482948</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC6430207/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Support for collection of datasets and samples was provided by the Collaborative Study on the Genetics of Alcoholism [COGA; U10 AA008401], the Collaborative Genetic Study of Nicotine Dependence [COGEND; P01 CA089392; see also phs000404.v1.p1], and the Family Study of Cocaine Dependence [FSCD; R01 DA013423, R01 DA019963].</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001745"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A family study of cocaine dependence at Washington University School of Medicine in St. Louis, Missouri, in which adults with cocaine dependence recruited from chemical dependency treatment centres in the St. Louis area, and community controls matched to them on age, ethnicity, gender and zip code and recruited through driver&apos;s licence records, each took part with a full sibling, to study family, neighbourhood and genetic contributions to substance dependence; recruitment targeted equal numbers of men and women and of European-Americans and African-Americans.</owl:annotatedTarget>
