    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001811 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001811">
        <obo:IAO_0100001 rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000165"/>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Obsoleted 2026-09-29. P1: GLC is one of six lung-cancer case-control GWAS combined in a TRICL-consortium meta-analysis of a DNA-repair-gene variant; P6: GLC is one of four studies pooled in a gene-set-analysis meta-analysis. No fetched evidence text for P2-P5 to confirm GLC&apos;s role there. The Transdisciplinary Research in Cancer of the Lung consortium (TRICL, COHO:0000800), named in P1&apos;s title as the framework combining GLC with other studies, is not in this name&apos;s candidate list, so memberOf is not asserted; a researcher could add it. Checked 2026-09-25: The draft defined GLC only by the other GWAS it was meta-analysed with and never said who is in it. GLC is a GWAS set drawn from three named studies (PMC5530424: &apos;We considered the German Lung Cancer study (GLC) with 488 cases and 478 controls, based on the data of participants taken from the following three individual studies: Lung Cancer in the Young (LUCY), a population-based multicentre study run by the Helmholtz Zentrum Munich, and the University Medical Centre of the Georg-August-University in Goettingen.&apos; ... &apos;A subset of the study participants of these three studies was chosen to form the German Lung Cancer GWAS.&apos;), the other two being the Heidelberg lung cancer case-control study and KORA. Made an aggregation, as for GLAUGEN (E429) and GGAF (E419) in this batch. Its parent TRICL (COHO:0000800), named in P1&apos;s title, was not asserted; it is now, with a quote. Members for hasCohort links: LUCY (E650, placeholder COHO:0002020) and KORA (COHO:0000045), quote &apos;We considered the German Lung Cancer study (GLC) with 488 cases and 478 controls, based on the data of participants taken from the following three individual studies: Lung Cancer in the Young (LUCY), a population-based multicentre study run by the Helmholtz Zentrum Munich, and the University Medical Centre of the Georg-August-University in Goettingen.&apos; and &apos;Additional controls were provided by Cooperative Health Research in the Augsburg Region (KORA), a population-based genome-wide study carried out by the Helmholtz Zentrum Munich [ 44 ].&apos; (https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC5530424/fullTextXML); the Heidelberg lung cancer case-control study is not in COHO. In the 2012 TRICL meta-analysis the same set is called &apos;HGF Germany&apos; / &apos;the Helmholtz-Gemeinschaft Deutscher Forschungszentren (HGF) lung cancer GWAS&apos;, a name that could be added as a synonym. If the curator prefers such pooled GWAS sets as cohorts, keep the definition and drop the members. Country Germany from the name, as drafted. Quotes from PMC5530424, PMC5342225 and PMC5429754 were read from live Europe PMC fetches. Completed 2026-09-29: Duplicate of COHO:0000165 (&apos;The Helmholtz-Gemeinschaft Deutscher Forschungszentren&apos;, HGF, HMGU), whose definition since 2026-09-25 is this same German lung cancer GWAS set. The evidence: the ILCCO table of PMID:28273134 gives &apos;GLCd 487 480 Germany Population-based case–control (&lt;50 years) HumanHap550K 503 381&apos; with footnote &apos;German Lung Cancer Study&apos;, the same cases, controls, design, platform and SNP count as TRICL 2012&apos;s row &apos;HGF Germanyg 487 480 Germany Population-based case–control (&lt;50 years) 550 K 503 381&apos; (PMID:22899653); both, and PMID:28785300 and PMID:26501144 (&apos;484 Caucasians used as controls in the German Lung Cancer Study&apos;, the HMGU controls of PMID:25644374), describe it as LUCY and Heidelberg cases against KORA controls, and Landi 2009 and PMID:26501144 cite the same design paper (Sauter 2008, PMID:18483334) for the HGF GWAS and the German Lung Cancer Study. The PGS Catalog simply follows each paper&apos;s name: HGF for PMID:22899653 and 26363033, HMGU for 25644374, GLC for 27288692 and 28604730. All the record&apos;s names (GLC, German Lung Cancer Study, Germany Lung Cancer study, German Lung Cancer GWAS, German lung cancer study) are names of that set and can be carried; each was found in the cache (PMID:27288692, 28785300, 26501144, 28273134). The record&apos;s example and TRICL membership quotes are verbatim; the TRICL membership is given in links for COHO:0000165 (it has only ILCCO now). Optional further synonyms for COHO:0000165, seen verbatim: &apos;HGF Germany&apos; (PMID:22899653) and &apos;Helmholtz-Gemeinschaft Deutscher Forschungszentren Lung Cancer GWAS&apos; (Landi 2009 Table 1 footnote). LUCY&apos;s record named this placeholder as its parent; see the LUCY line (COHO:0002020) for how that is handled. Replaced by COHO:0000165 (The Helmholtz-Gemeinschaft Deutscher Forschungszentren), which now carries this term&apos;s names. Found by the checks of the placeholders&apos; names against their sources recorded in the pipeline repository, completed 2026-09-29.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">obsolete German Lung Cancer Study</rdfs:label>
        <owl:deprecated rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</owl:deprecated>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001812 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001812">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <rdf:type rdf:nodeID="genid16157"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Asia"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Denmark"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Europe"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Israel"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Northern_America"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Sweden"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_Kingdom"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <coho:isCohortOf rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001813"/>
        <coho:isCohortOf rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002720"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:22037877</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An international family study of non-syndromic glioma run by the Gliogene Consortium (Genetic Epidemiology of Glioma International Consortium) to find glioma susceptibility genes, first by genome-wide linkage analysis: between 2007 and 2011 its 14 centres in the United States, Sweden, Denmark, the United Kingdom and Israel recruited families with at least two pathologically confirmed intracranial primary gliomas among first- to third-degree relatives, found by screening glioma patients in clinics, by review of medical records or through the internet, and collected questionnaires from probands and blood or saliva samples from living affected relatives.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116 xml:lang="en">The acronym Gliogene Consortium also denotes Genetic Epidemiology of Glioma International Consortium (COHO:0002720); this term is Gliogene study.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000116 xml:lang="en">The acronym Gliogene also denotes Genetic Epidemiology of Glioma International Consortium (COHO:0002720); this term is Gliogene study.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <coho:numberOfCases rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">841</coho:numberOfCases>
        <coho:numberOfParticipants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">9797</coho:numberOfParticipants>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>GLIOGENE</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>GLIOGENE study</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Gliogene</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Gliogene Consortium</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
