    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001843"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:nodeID="genid16399"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>Alzheimer&apos;s disease</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3337217/</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>HBS is a Harvard-wide, longitudinal, case-control study designed to accelerate the discovery and validation of molecular diagnostics that track or predict progression of early-stage PD and Alzheimer’s disease (AD).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001843"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:nodeID="genid16401"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>Parkinson&apos;s disease</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3337217/</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>HBS is a Harvard-wide, longitudinal, case-control study designed to accelerate the discovery and validation of molecular diagnostics that track or predict progression of early-stage PD and Alzheimer’s disease (AD).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001843"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41588-021-00847-6/MediaObjects/41588_2021_847_MOESM1_ESM.pdf</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>HBS (USA) Dementia was defined using operationalized level 1 MDS dementia criteria</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001843"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PGS000903</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Identification of novel risk loci, causal insights, and heritable risk for Parkinson&apos;s disease: a meta-analysis of genome-wide association studies.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:31701892</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001843"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:31701892</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Identification of novel risk loci, causal insights, and heritable risk for Parkinson&apos;s disease: a meta-analysis of genome-wide association studies.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:31701892</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/research/bionlp/RESTful/pmcoa.cgi/BioC_xml/PMC8422160/unicode</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>To determine the proportion of SNP-based heritability explained by our PD GWAS results using PRS, we used a two-stage design, with variant selection and training in the NeuroX-dbGaP dataset (5,851 cases and 5,866 controls) and then validation in the Harvard Biomarker Study (HBS, 527 cases and 472 controls).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001843"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A longitudinal biomarker cohort study run jointly by Brigham and Women&apos;s Hospital and Massachusetts General Hospital in Boston, following patients with Parkinson&apos;s disease, patients with other neurodegenerative diseases and healthy controls at structured in-person study visits with biobanked samples, to accelerate the discovery and validation of molecular diagnostic and progression markers of early-stage Parkinson&apos;s disease.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:32250320</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:38480700</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC8060834/fullTextXML</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001843"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/numberOfCases"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">933</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>Parkinson&apos;s disease cases and controls in the 2026 health-phenome analysis (analysed subset; no total for the whole study stated)</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A42579898+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Our objective is to evaluate the complexity of features associated with PD in a study of 933 cases and 291 controls in the Harvard Biomarkers Study.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001843"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/numberOfControls"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">291</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>Parkinson&apos;s disease cases and controls in the 2026 health-phenome analysis (analysed subset; no total for the whole study stated)</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A42579898+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Our objective is to evaluate the complexity of features associated with PD in a study of 933 cases and 291 controls in the Harvard Biomarkers Study.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
