    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002033 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002033">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <rdf:type rdf:nodeID="genid17755"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Australia"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Oceania"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PGS000334</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:32968074</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A longitudinal cohort of non-demented, community-dwelling people aged 70 to 90, recruited by random approach from the electoral roll in two areas of Sydney, Australia, and begun in 2005 to examine the clinical characteristics and prevalence of mild cognitive impairment and related syndromes and the rate of change in cognitive function over time, with annual telephone reviews and periodic detailed neuropsychiatric and medical assessments.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116 xml:lang="en">The acronym MAS also denotes German Multicenter Allergy Study (COHO:0001178) and Malaysian Ovarian Cancer Genetic Study (COHO:0002103); this term is Sydney Memory and Ageing Study.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <coho:numberOfParticipants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1037</coho:numberOfParticipants>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MAS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Sydney MAS</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#PGS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Sydney Memory and Ageing Study</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Restriction rdf:nodeID="genid17755">
        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/is_population_study_of"/>
        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0002039"/>
    </owl:Restriction>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002033"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:nodeID="genid17755"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>mild cognitive impairment</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A20637138+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Sydney Memory and Ageing Study (Sydney MAS) was initiated in 2005 to examine the clinical characteristics and prevalence of mild cognitive impairment (MCI) and related syndromes, and to determine the rate of change in cognitive function over time.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002033"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Australia"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A21491632+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Sydney Memory and Ageing Study, Australia.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002033"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PGS000334</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Risk prediction of late-onset Alzheimer&apos;s disease implies an oligogenic architecture.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:32968074</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002033"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:32968074</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Risk prediction of late-onset Alzheimer&apos;s disease implies an oligogenic architecture.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:32968074</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC7511365/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>We evaluated the performance of GRSfull using samples from the Australian Imaging, Biomarker &amp; Lifestyle Study (AIBL, 216 cases and 631 controls), the Sydney Memory and Ageing study (Sydney MAS, 77 cases and 588 controls) and the UKB (383 cases and 1915 controls) (Table 1).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002033"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A longitudinal cohort of non-demented, community-dwelling people aged 70 to 90, recruited by random approach from the electoral roll in two areas of Sydney, Australia, and begun in 2005 to examine the clinical characteristics and prevalence of mild cognitive impairment and related syndromes and the rate of change in cognitive function over time, with annual telephone reviews and periodic detailed neuropsychiatric and medical assessments.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:20637138</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002033"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/numberOfParticipants"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1037</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>non-demented community-dwelling people aged 70-90 recruited at baseline (2005)</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A20637138+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Non-demented community-dwelling individuals (N = 1037) aged 70-90 were recruited from two areas of Sydney, following a random approach to 8914 individuals on the electoral roll.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002033"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>MAS</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002034 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002034">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0000000"/>
        <coho:hasSubCohort rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0001858"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Northern_America"/>
        <coho:has_data_collection_location rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PMID:10920470</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A population-based registry of twins and their families, primarily born or living in Virginia, North Carolina and South Carolina and ascertained mainly from birth records (and formerly school records), formed in 1997 by merging the Virginia Twin Registry (established around 1975) with the North Carolina Twin Registry, with South Carolina added in 1998; based at Virginia Commonwealth University, it recruits its twins into research studies, collects data and DNA, and keeps a DNA repository.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <coho:numberOfParticipants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">60000</coho:numberOfParticipants>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>MATR</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Mid Atlantic Twin Registry</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Mid-Atlantic Twin Registry of Virginia Commonwealth University</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Mid-Atlantic Twin Registry</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002034"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A12537875+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Mid-Atlantic Twin Registry (MATR) is a population-based registry of twin pairs ascertained from birth records and school system records of Virginia, North Carolina, and South Carolina.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002034"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:10920470</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Sex-specific genetic influences on the comorbidity of alcoholism and major depression in a population-based sample of US twins.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:10920470</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A10920470+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Lifetime major depression (MD), alcohol abuse, and alcohol dependence were assessed by structured interview for both members of 3755 twin pairs from the Mid-Atlantic Twin Registry.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002034"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A population-based registry of twins and their families, primarily born or living in Virginia, North Carolina and South Carolina and ascertained mainly from birth records (and formerly school records), formed in 1997 by merging the Virginia Twin Registry (established around 1975) with the North Carolina Twin Registry, with South Carolina added in 1998; based at Virginia Commonwealth University, it recruits its twins into research studies, collects data and DNA, and keeps a DNA repository.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:12537875</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:23218199</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:31608855</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002034"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/numberOfParticipants"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">60000</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>twins registered in the registry by 2019 (stated as a lower bound, &apos;more than 60,000&apos;)</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://doi.org/10.1017/thg.2019.87</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The Mid-Atlantic Twin Registry (MATR) is a population-based registry of more than 60,000 twins primarily born or living in Virginia, North Carolina and South Carolina.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002034"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>MATR</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasSynonymType rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OMO_0003000"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


