    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002257"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:nodeID="genid19332"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>bipolar disorder</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3729260/</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The initial 10,000 GPC participants drawn from earlier studies include our Portuguese Island Collection (PIC) of individuals and multiplex families with schizophrenia and/or bipolar disorder.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002257"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:nodeID="genid19334"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>schizophrenia</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3729260/</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The initial 10,000 GPC participants drawn from earlier studies include our Portuguese Island Collection (PIC) of individuals and multiplex families with schizophrenia and/or bipolar disorder.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002257"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/Portugal"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=EXT_ID%3A15704217+AND+SRC%3AMED&amp;format=json&amp;resultType=core</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Specifically, the 25 families included 20 families from the Portuguese island collection (PIC; 15 families from the Azores Islands and 5 from the Madeira Islands) and 5 families from continental Portugal.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002257"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_data_collection_location"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://dbpedia.org/resource/United_States"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fnature07239/MediaObjects/41586_2008_BFnature07239_MOESM238_ESM.pdf</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>This population consisted of subjects living in Portugal, the Azorean and Madeiran islands, or the direct (first or second generation) Portuguese immigrant population in the United States</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002257"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000112"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:24123842</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Association study of 83 candidate genes for bipolar disorder in chromosome 6q selected using an evidence-based prioritization algorithm.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:24123842</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12888025/</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Trios, nuclear families—defined as two parents and one or more children, of which at least one child was affected—and phenotypically-discordant sibling pairs were selected from the Portuguese Island Collection (PIC) and the NIMH Genetics Initiative Bipolar Pedigrees on the basis of a proband meeting criteria for bipolar I disorder (BPI), bipolar II disorder (BPII), or schizoaffective disorder, bipolar type.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002257"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A collection of individuals and multiplex families with schizophrenia and/or bipolar disorder, with psychiatrically screened controls, sampled from the founder population of the Azores and Madeira archipelagos of Portugal through all the islands&apos; psychiatric and general hospitals, taking families only if the proband&apos;s parents and four grandparents were native to the islands, with further families from mainland Portugal identified through the University of Coimbra and participants from the first- and second-generation Portuguese immigrant population of the United States; it was used in linkage and association studies of psychosis and became part of the Genomic Psychiatry Cohort.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:15704217</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:23650244</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>PMID:24123842</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fnature07239/MediaObjects/41586_2008_BFnature07239_MOESM238_ESM.pdf</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:contributor>Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09</dcterms:contributor>
