        <obo:IAO_0000112>GCST90492734</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000112>PGS012530</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An international initiative that assembled genome-wide association studies of type 2 diabetes from studies in six ancestry groups (African American, East Asian, European, Hispanic, South African and South Asian), among them the DIAMANTE Consortium and the Million Veteran Program, and meta-analysed them to characterise the genetic contribution to type 2 diabetes and its aetiological heterogeneity across populations.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <coho:numberOfCases rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">428452</coho:numberOfCases>
        <coho:numberOfControls rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2107149</coho:numberOfControls>
        <coho:numberOfParticipants rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2535601</coho:numberOfParticipants>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>T2D-GGI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>T2DGGI</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:inSubset rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho#PGS_subset"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Type 2 Diabetes Global Genomics Initiative</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    <owl:Restriction rdf:nodeID="genid20602">
        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/has_patients_with"/>
        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0005148"/>
    </owl:Restriction>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002430"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:nodeID="genid20602"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>type 2 diabetes</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC10937372/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>Through the newly established Type 2 Diabetes Global Genomics Initiative, we present findings from a very large meta-analysis of T2D GWAS data, comprising more than 2.5 million individuals of diverse ancestry—an increase of nearly threefold in the effective sample size compared with previous efforts8,9.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
