        <owl:annotatedTarget>PMID:24096698</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dcterms:title>Genome-wide association study of endometrial cancer in E2C2.</dcterms:title>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:24096698</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3898362/fullTextXML</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <rdfs:comment>The fast-track replication was conducted in a multiethnic sample of 2,294 cases and 3,395 controls from two cohorts [MEC and the Prevention Study II (CPSII) Nutrition cohort] and five case–control studies [the Alberta Health Services (AHS) study, FHCRC, Estrogen, Diet, Genetics, and Endometrial Cancer (EDGE) study, Turin, and Women’s Insights and Shared Experiences (WISE)] (Table 1).</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://www.ebi.ac.uk/coho/COHO_0002574"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A population-based pair of case-control studies, one of endometrial cancer and one of breast cancer, among white and African American women aged 50 to 79 in the Philadelphia area of Pennsylvania and New Jersey, United States, examining exogenous hormone use, parity and estrogen-related genes: cases diagnosed between July 1999 and June 2002 were identified through hospitals and the Pennsylvania Cancer Registry, and controls from the same area by random-digit dialling. Its endometrial cancer study is one of the studies of the Epidemiology of Endometrial Cancer Consortium.</owl:annotatedTarget>
